From: Monica Patel <monica.patel@redwood.com>
To: Vivek Kulkarni <vivek.kulkarni@redwood.com>, Vanessa Ortiz <vanessa.ortiz@redwood.com>, Grace O'Connor <grace.oconnor@redwood.com>
Cc: 
Date: 2026-11-22T14:27:00-08:00
Subject: Fwd: Connector resiliency & rate-fencing plan for BrightPay integration

Forwarding Sam's numbers — please see below. Goals: get an internal runbook + one-page integrator guide (for BrightPay engineering) with sample SDK code and explicit retry/timeouts. Key constraints: EU region, bursts to 120 r/s, sustained ~35 r/s, client currently uses API keys and honors Retry-After. Maintenance window on Saturdays 02:00–04:00 UTC.

Suggested implementation (high-level):
1) Connection and timeouts:
   - TCP keepalive = enabled, keepalive interval = 60s.
   - Default request timeout = 15s. For streaming calls, use idle timeout = 120s.
2) Retries and backoff:
   - Retry on network errors, 429, 5xx. Max attempts = 5.
   - Backoff strategy: base 200ms * 2^attempt + full jitter. Example: sleep = random(0, base * 2^n).
   - Respect Retry-After header when present (convert to ms). If Retry-After > 30s, surface as a soft-failure to the app; do not retry silently.
3) Rate limit handling and circuit breaker:
   - Token-bucket client-side with burst capacity = 150 tokens, refill rate = sustained QPS (35/s) per API key as reported.
   - Circuit breaker: open when > 50% of requests are 5xx or 429 over a 30s window; half-open after 60s.
4) Endpoint migration pattern:
   - Shadow mode: send requests to both old and new endpoints (new is read-only for 48h), compare latencies and error rates.
   - Progressive rollout: 5% -> 25% -> 100% using our routing layer. Automatic rollback if error budget > 2% over a 10min window.
5) Batching & KV cache suggestions:
   - Where possible, use batched embed/lookup endpoints with a max batch = 16.
   - Implement prefix KV caching for repeated prompts to reduce token cost and latency.

Sample SDK snippets (include in the integrator doc):
- Node (fetch):
  const fetchWithRetry = async (url, opts, attempts = 0) => {
    try {
      const res = await fetch(url, opts);
      if (res.status === 429) {
        const ra = res.headers.get('Retry-After');
        if (ra) {
          await sleep(parseFloat(ra) * 1000);
        }
        throw new Error('Rate limited');
      }
      if (res.status >= 500 && attempts < 5) throw new Error('server');
      return res;
    } catch (e) {
      if (attempts >= 5) throw e;
      const backoff = Math.random() * (200 * Math.pow(2, attempts));
      await sleep(backoff);
      return fetchWithRetry(url, opts, attempts + 1);
    }
  };

- cURL example for parallel-run:
  curl -X POST 'https://api.redwood-inference.eu/v1/generate' 
    -H 'Authorization: ApiKey <KEY>' 
    -H 'Idempotency-Key: <id>' 
    -d '{"input":"hello"}'

Operational notes:
- Instrument per-route latency and error rate (SLO: p95 latency < 300ms for simple text generation).
- Add a lightweight telemetry tag (x-rd-integration: brightpay) for easy filtering in logs.

Can Vivek/Vanessa draft the integrator one-pager and the checklist? I suggest we include the code samples in the first cut and a troubleshooting section for common 429/timeout symptoms.

— Monica

On 2026-11-22 11:03, Sam Cooper wrote:
> Monica —
> 
> Thanks, this looks good. Quick answers:
> - Peak burst: we see short bursts up to ~120 r/s (1–2 minute spikes) and a sustained average around 35 r/s.
> - Auth: API keys for now; we may switch to client-credentials OAuth later this quarter.
> - Region: yes, eu-west-1 is preferred.
> - Maintenance: weekly Saturday window 02:00–04:00 UTC — avoid cutovers then.
> 
> Notes: our gateway currently returns standard 429 with Retry-After seconds and includes an X-Client-RateLimit-Remaining header. We can add a small header for idempotency if you need it.
> 
